finish('classical-randomized-polynomial-time-integer-factorization',
 id='TCS-6617', title='Can a classical randomized algorithm factor every integer in polynomial time?',
 area='Lattices and computational number theory', criterion='resources', question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Do there exist constants C,c and a uniform classical randomized algorithm that, on every positive integer N given in binary, outputs its complete prime factorization with probability at least 2/3 and always halts within C(n+1)ᶜ bit operations, where n=⌊log₂N⌋+1?''',
 definitions=r'''For N≥2, the required output is the ordered list (p₁,e₁),…,(pᵣ,eᵣ), with distinct primes p₁<⋯<pᵣ, positive integer exponents eᵢ, and N=∏ᵢpᵢ^{eᵢ}. All output integers are encoded in binary. The factorization of N=1 is the empty list; zero and malformed encodings are outside the positive-integer domain and are rejected. The same finite probabilistic Turing machine must work for all N. Its random tape contains independent fair bits, and the success probability is over these bits separately for each fixed input. The polynomial time bound holds on every random tape. An incorrect output or a failure symbol counts as failure. There are no quantum operations, input-dependent advice, factoring or order-finding oracles, promises of a special form for N, or supplied factors of nearby integers. In particular n is the bit length of N, not its numerical value.''',
 answer_criterion='Answer yes with a proved algorithm meeting the worst-case polynomial running time and per-input success guarantee. Answer no by proving that every uniform classical randomized algorithm fails one of these requirements for some inputs, regardless of its polynomial exponent. Deterministic factoring would suffice for yes, but a deterministic lower bound alone would not suffice for no. Experiments or algorithms for a restricted class of integers do not settle the statement.',
 context=[
  paragraph('Multiplying the prime factors is easy, and the factors themselves fit in a polynomial-size output. Discovering them is the unresolved step. Trial division up to √N is polynomial in the number’s value but exponential in its binary input length. The distinction between N and log N is essential even when a newer algorithm improves the power of N substantially.'),
  paragraph('Primality testing and factorization are different tasks. The Agrawal–Kayal–Saxena algorithm decides in deterministic polynomial time whether a number is prime, without necessarily exhibiting a divisor when the answer is composite. It also makes a proposed full factorization efficiently checkable: verify that every listed base is prime and that the product is N.','primality'),
  paragraph('Because an output can be checked, a bounded-error polynomial-time factoring algorithm can be repeated until a valid factorization is obtained, giving an always-correct algorithm with polynomial expected time. Conversely, a polynomial expected-time always-correct algorithm can be truncated at a suitable constant multiple of its expected bound to obtain the formulation above. This equivalence concerns performance on every input; success on a sampled family of integers alone is weaker.'),
  paragraph('The general number field sieve has the familiar heuristic expected-time estimate exp(((64/9)^{1/3}+o(1))(ln N)^{1/3}(ln ln N)^{2/3}). This is subexponential but still superpolynomial in the bit length. Rigorous deterministic bounds of N^{1/5+o(1)} make a different tradeoff and remain exponential in that length. Neither bound is a proof that faster classical factoring is impossible.','orders'),
  paragraph('Shor’s quantum algorithm factors in polynomial time, using quantum order finding. It provides an upper bound in a more powerful computational model; it does not establish a classical lower bound. A classical polynomial-time algorithm would undermine factoring-based cryptographic assumptions, but the absence of such an algorithm has not been proved from P≠NP or from the efficiency of multiplication.','shor')],
 why='Integer factorization is a basic inverse problem in arithmetic and a central test case for the potential power of quantum computation. Its classical complexity has direct consequences for factoring-based cryptography and for algorithms that currently require access to a factoring subroutine.',
 importance=dict(score=99, method='editorial', assessed_on='2026-09-10', reason='A defining open problem of computational number theory, with broad consequences for cryptography, arithmetic algorithms, and classical-versus-quantum computation.'),
 progress=[
  step('1980s–1990s','Sieve methods establish powerful subexponential classical approaches. The standard general number field sieve estimate remains a heuristic expected-time analysis, as summarized in the current large-order paper.','orders'),
  step('1994–1997','Shor gives polynomial-time quantum algorithms for factorization and discrete logarithms. Quantum computation is outside the classical model fixed here.','shor'),
  step('2002–2004','Agrawal, Kayal, and Saxena prove deterministic polynomial-time primality testing. The result does not give a polynomial-time factoring algorithm.','primality'),
  step('2021–2022','Harvey and Hittmeir rigorously factor N in O(N^{1/5}(log N)^{16/5}/(log log N)^{3/5}) deterministic bit operations, improving the preceding exponent-one-fifth bound by a logarithmic factor.','deterministic'),
  step('5 June 2026','Harvey and Hittmeir’s revised large-order algorithm removes earlier power-of-N lower restrictions on its target order parameter. Their introduction still explicitly identifies polynomial-time integer factorization as unknown; the improved subroutine does not provide such a factoring algorithm.','orders')],
 references=[
  ref('survey','A Survey of Techniques Used in Algebraic and Number Theoretic Algorithms','Manindra Agrawal; Chandan Saha',None,'https://www.csa.iisc.ac.in/~chandan/research/survey_CNT.pdf','Author-hosted survey; introduction, complexity of arithmetic operations, and smooth-number factoring techniques; supplied PDF has no publication date on its title page'),
  ref('primality','PRIMES is in P','Manindra Agrawal; Neeraj Kayal; Nitin Saxena',2004,'https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2004/160-2/p12','Annals of Mathematics 160(2), 781–793; polynomial-time primality testing'),
  ref('shor','Polynomial-Time Algorithms for Prime Factorization and Discrete Logarithms on a Quantum Computer','Peter W. Shor',1997,'https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9508027','Full version of the FOCS 1994 result; arXiv revision 25 January 1996; SIAM Journal on Computing 26(5), 1484–1509'),
  ref('deterministic','A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation','David Harvey; Markus Hittmeir',2022,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11105','Preprint 24 May 2021; Mathematics of Computation, DOI 10.1090/mcom/3708; complete factorization bound in bit operations'),
  ref('orders','Deterministic methods for finding elements of large multiplicative order','David Harvey; Markus Hittmeir',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11131v2','Revision 5 June 2026; Sections 1.1–1.2 distinguish heuristic sieve bounds, deterministic factoring, and the improved order-finding subroutine')],
 status='source_open', status_note='Explicitly identified as unknown in Harvey and Hittmeir’s June 2026 paper. Research through 10 September 2026 found no proved classical polynomial-time algorithm for all integers or impossibility theorem for the stated randomized model. Numerical scaling evidence and quantum algorithms do not establish either answer.', formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
